(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["ce-easy-2026-010"]={"v":1,"id":"ce-easy-2026-010","title":"Full Syllabus Test-1 2026 ESE CE General Studies & Engineering Aptitude","sourceTitle":"Full Syllabus Test-1 2026 ESE CE General Studies & Engineering Aptitude","branch":"CIVIL ENGINEERING","code":"CE","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 + ESE 2026 Online Test Series - Civil Engineering","seriesId":"ce-easy-2026","num":10,"category":"aptitude","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":7200,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + ESE 2026 Online Test Series - Civil Engineering • Questions: 100","package":"GATE 2026 + ESE 2026 Online Test Series - Civil Engineering"},"source":"CIVIL ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE 2026 + ESE 2026 Online Test Series - Civil Engineering/010_Full Syllabus Test-1 2026 ESE CE General Studies & Engineering Aptitude.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Jan Vishwas (Amendment of Provisions) Bill, 2025 provides for</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Criminalization of minor offences"},{"key":"B","html":"Suspension of MPs upon charge-sheet"},{"key":"C","html":"Automatic revision of fines by 10% every 3 years"},{"key":"D","html":"Direct impeachment of Ministers"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nFines are revised upward by 10% every 3 years to maintain deterrence.</p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>With reference to the National Sports Governance Act, 2025, consider the following statements:<br />\n1. It provides for National sports board (NSB) to grant recognition to sports bodies.<br />\n2. It establishes a National Sports Tribunal to adjudicate sports-related disputes.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nNSB recognizes bodies, affiliates, and only recognized bodies get central funds; it is also under<br />\nRTI.</p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following countries are members of ASEAN?<br />\n1. Indonesia<br />\n2. Thailand<br />\n3. Australia<br />\n4. Philippines<br />\nChoose the correct option given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 2 only"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n10 Members of ASEAN: Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, and Vietnam.</p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding Sovereign Credit Ratings (SCR):<br />\n1. Higher sovereign ratings reduce borrowing costs and facilitate foreign investment.<br />\n2. Sovereign ratings are legally binding for a country’s borrowing.<br />\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nHigher ratings reduce default risk, borrowing costs, and attract investment. Statement 2 is Incorrect.</p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The Adi Karmayogi Abhiyan aims to develop a cadre of tribal leaders primarily to achieve which of the following outcomes in tribal areas?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Industrialization of tribal lands"},{"key":"B","html":"100% saturation of government services"},{"key":"C","html":"Privatization of tribal resources"},{"key":"D","html":"Migration of tribals to urban centers"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n100% saturation of government services like road connectivity and Ayushman Bharat.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Regarding India’s Ethanol Blending Programme, consider the following statements:<br />\n1. India has achieved its 20% ethanol blending target in petrol by 2025.<br />\n2. The National Policy on Biofuels (2018), as amended in 2022, advanced the 20% blending target to 2025- 26 from 2030.<br />\n3. 1st Generation Ethanol is primarily made from lignocellulosic biomass or agricultural residues.<br />\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Union Minister for Petroleum and Natural Gas announced India&rsquo;s achievement of 20% Ethanol blending target in petrol by 2025.<br />\n&bull; The National Policy on Biofuels (2018), as amended in 2022, advanced the target of 20% blending of ethanol in petrol to 2025- 26 from 2030.<br />\n&bull; 1st Generation Ethanol is made from food crops such as cereals, sugarcane, sugar beet, etc.<br />\n2<sup>nd</sup> Generation Ethanol is made from ligno-cellulosic or woody biomass, or agricultural residues.</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>With reference to the Maratha Military Landscapes of India, recently added to the UNESCO<br />\nWorld Heritage List, consider the following statements:<br />\n1. The Maratha Military Landscapes are located exclusively in the state of Maharashtra.<br />\n2. The Gingee Hill Fort, located in Tamil Nadu, is one of the sites included in this designation.<br />\n3. Raigad Hill Fort, selected by Chhatrapati Shivaji for his permanent capital, is part of this landscape.<br />\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; The Maratha Military Landscapes are geographically spread across both Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu.<br />\n&bull; The Gingee Hill Fort, located in Tamil Nadu, is explicitly listed as one of the twelve forts within the Maratha Military Landscapes.<br />\n&bull; Raigad Hill Fort was selected by Chhatrapati Shivaji as his permanent capital, and it is indeed part of the Maratha Military Landscapes.</p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Vizhinjam International Seaport is developed under the landlord model with DBFOT basis.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> It is India’s first shallow-draft transshipment port.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"B","html":"Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I)."},{"key":"C","html":"Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false."},{"key":"D","html":"Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nVizhinjam is a deep-draft port.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding definition of ‘Moral’:<br />\n1. Being or acting in accordance with standards and precepts of goodness.<br />\n2. Arising from conscience or a sense of right and wrong.<br />\n3. A concisely expressed perception or general truth.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>“Double Dipping” is related to:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Copyrights"},{"key":"B","html":"Risk"},{"key":"C","html":"Collegiality"},{"key":"D","html":"Conflict of interest"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nDouble Dipping is a situation where an engineer can be compensated by more than one party for services on the same project.</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Psychological courage is bravery inherent in facing one’s inner demons.<br />\n2. Courage involves an element of fearlessness.<br />\n3. Courageous action may be voluntary or by coercive approach<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; Courage involves element of mastery on fear. Hence statement 2 is incorrect<br />\n&bull; Courageous action must be voluntarily. Hence statement 3 is incorrect</p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements about Bootlegging:<br />\n1. Bootlegging is usually bottom-up, non-programmed activity without the official permission of the responsible management.<br />\n2. Bootlegging is an illegitimate behaviour which may cause an ethical dilemma between moral imperatives.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Humility is an example of public-spirited virtue.<br />\n2. Self-direction virtues are fundamental in exercising moral autonomy and responsibility.<br />\n3. The ability to communicate effectively in an example of team-work virtue.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which ethical theory is centered around the ideal of the ‘categorical imperative’?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Moral Relativism"},{"key":"B","html":"Deontology"},{"key":"C","html":"Virtue Ethics"},{"key":"D","html":"Consequentialism"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nDeontology is an ethical theory that uses rules to distinguish right from wrong. Deontology is often associated with philosopher Immanuel Kant.</p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The ethical theory of ‘egoism’ suggests that individuals should:<br />\n1. Pursue their own self-interests.<br />\n2. Follow universal moral rules.<br />\n3. Act in the best interest of others.<br />\nSelect the correct answer using the codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nEthical egoism is the normative theory that the promotion of one&rsquo;s own good is in accordance with morality.</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Building a sustainable environment includes:<br />\n1. Developing a green supply chain.<br />\n2. Omitting hazardous emissions.<br />\n3. Following the codes of ethics.<br />\nSelect the correct answer using the codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Confidentiality simply means not sharing client information.<br />\n2. A whistleblower is a person who reveals any policy of an organization to the public.<br />\n3. Trust has the common element of belief, mutuality and predictability.<br />\n4. The moral values held by an individual are enduring or lasting belief.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nA whistleblower is a person who reveals wrong doing of an organization to the public.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>With reference to the marine ecosystem, consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Aphotic zone is an area of inky darkness, which occupies the great bulk of the ocean.<br />\n2. The neritic province has high nutrient levels due to dissolved materials in riverine runoff.<br />\n3. The euphotic zone is the uppermost layer of a body of water, like an ocean or lake, that receives enough sunlight for photosynthesis to occur.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding the sources of Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs):<br />\n1. Elevated temperatures and combustion processes can create POPs.<br />\n2. Improper use and disposal of agrochemicals can release POPs into the environment.<br />\n3. Unwanted by-products of industrial processes can produce POPs as byproducts.<br />\nSelect the correct answer using the codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n<strong>&bull; Elevated temperatures and combustion processes:</strong> These can unintentionally create POPs,<br />\nsuch as dioxins and furans, as by-products of incomplete combustion.<br />\n<strong>&bull; Improper use and disposal of agrochemicals:</strong> Certain pesticides, like DDT, are POPs, and<br />\ntheir improper handling can release them into the environment.<br />\n<strong>&bull; Unwanted by-products of industrial processes:</strong> Many industrial activities inadvertently<br />\nproduce POPs as by-products, which are then released into the environment</p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is not taken into consideration for National Air Quality Index (NAQI)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ammonia"},{"key":"B","html":"Ozone"},{"key":"C","html":"Methane"},{"key":"D","html":"Nitrogen Dioxide"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) and methane are not taken into consideration for the National Air<br />\nQuality Index (NAQI).<br />\n&bull; The index focuses on eight primary pollutants, viz. PM10, PM2.5, Nitrogen Dioxide (NO<sub>2</sub>),<br />\nSulphur Dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>), Carbon Monoxide (CO), Ozone (O<sub>3</sub>), Ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>), and Lead (Pb).</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In which of the following process there is an increase in concentration of a toxin as it moves from one trophic level to the next?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Bioaccumulation"},{"key":"B","html":"Biomagnification"},{"key":"C","html":"Bioconcentration"},{"key":"D","html":"Biological Spectrum"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN):<br />\n1. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species, is the world’s most comprehensive inventory of the global conservation status of plant and animal species.<br />\n2. Recently, IUCN published the first Global Tree Assessment, as part of an update to the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species,<br />\n3. Its headquarters are in Gland, Switzerland.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is the largest National Park in India, in terms of area?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Hemis National Park"},{"key":"B","html":"Valmiki National Park"},{"key":"C","html":"Jim Corbett National Park"},{"key":"D","html":"Ranthambore National Park"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nHemis National Park is the largest National Park in India covering an area of 4400 km<sup><sub>2</sub></sup>.</p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding Methane emissions:<br />\n1. Over a 20-year period, methane is less potent at global warming than carbon dioxide.<br />\n2. Methane is one of the primary contributors to the formation of ground-level ozone.<br />\n3. Methane remains in the atmosphere for much longer than Carbon dioxide.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Over a 20-year period, methane is 80 times more potent at warming than carbon dioxide,<br />\naccording to a report by the United Nations Environment Programme.<br />\n&bull; Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) remains in the atmosphere for significantly longer than methane (CH<sub>4</sub>).<br />\nWhile methane is a much more potent greenhouse gas, its average atmospheric lifetime is<br />\nabout a decade. In contrast, CO<sub>2</sub> can persist in the atmosphere for centuries or even thousands<br />\nof years, leading to a long-term increase in its atmospheric concentration.</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Green hydrogen is produced when natural gas is split into hydrogen and carbon dioxide.<br />\n2. Blue hydrogen is produced when hydrogen is produced by splitting water by electrolysis.<br />\n3. Brown hydrogen is formed through coal gasification.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Blue hydrogen is produced when natural gas is split into hydrogen and carbon dioxide.<br />\n&bull; Green hydrogen is produced when hydrogen is produced by splitting water by electrolysis.<br />\n&bull; Brown hydrogen is formed through coal gasification.</p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>“State of World Forest” report is released by which of the following organizations?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN)"},{"key":"B","html":"Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO)"},{"key":"C","html":"World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF)"},{"key":"D","html":"United Nation Environment Programme (UNEP)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe State of the World&rsquo;s Forests report is a flagship publication by the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) of the United Nations that provides global data and analysis on the current condition of the world&rsquo;s forests.</p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. QR code was originally developed for process optimization in logistics of the automotive industry.<br />\n2. QR technology is an “open source” technology.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nOriginally developed by the Japanese company Denso Wave in 1994, QR codes were designed to<br />\nstreamline logistics in the automotive industry. QR Code is extensively used in automotive and<br />\nmanufacturing for part tracking and logistics optimization.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Cache memory acts as a buffer between the CPU and main memory.<br />\n2. Random Access Memory (RAM) is a temporary storage that processors use to store programs, while they are running.<br />\n3. Primary Memory is non-volatile memory that is permanently built.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nPrimary memory, also known as main memory or RAM (Random Access Memory), is typically<br />\nvolatile i.e. it stores data temporarily and loses data when the power is turned off.</p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Python is a dynamic, interpreted (bytecode-compiled) language.<br />\n2. Python is commonly used for developing websites and software, task automation, data analysis, and data visualization.<br />\n3. Python runs on an interpreter system, meaning that code can be executed as soon as it is written.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>WiMax is a wireless communication technology based on</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"PAN"},{"key":"B","html":"WAN"},{"key":"C","html":"MAN"},{"key":"D","html":"LAN"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nWiMax stands for Worldwide Interoperability for Microwave Access. This technology is based<br />\non IEEE 802.16 standard. It is used to provide higher data rates with increased coverage. It is<br />\nbased on MAN (Metropolitan Area Network) technology and range is up to 50 km.</p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements about OSI model:<br />\n1. The physical layer is responsible for carrying a raw bit stream over a physical media.<br />\n2. The physical link layer provides a flow control mechanism in case data rate absorbed by receiver is less than rate produced at sender.<br />\n3. Medium Access Control (MAC) is a sublayer in the physical layer that deals with the issue of<br />\naccess control to shared channels.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2 and 3 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe data link layer consists of two sublayers: the Media Access Control (MAC) sublayer and the<br />\nLogical Link Control (LLC) sublayer. The MAC sublayer handles the physical addressing and<br />\nmanages access to the shared medium, while the LLC sublayer handles flow control, error<br />\nchecking, and multiplexing protocols. Hence, statements 2 and 3 are not correct.</p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements about Network Topologies:<br />\n1. In Mesh Topology, there is a dedicated point to link from one device to every other device.<br />\n2. In Star Topology, each terminal is connected to the next terminal with the last one connected to the first in the form of a ring.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIn star topology, each node is connected directly to a central network hub or switch.</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is not an input unit?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Projector"},{"key":"B","html":"Trackball"},{"key":"C","html":"OMR"},{"key":"D","html":"Barcode reader"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Parallel transmission is common especially where the distance between the two devices is long.<br />\n2. Serial transmission uses one line and transits all bits along it one after the other.<br />\n3. Serial transmission is suitable for long distance communication.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2 and 3 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nParallel transmission is common especially where the distance between the two devices is short.</p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If the radius of generating circle is 20% of the radius of directing circle. Then the number of epicycloid formed for a complete revolution of generating circle will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"5"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb07111679d0d3b77647.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>What should be the position of object with respect to picture plane in order to obtain an enlarged perspective view?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Object should be behind picture plane"},{"key":"B","html":"Object should be in front of picture plane"},{"key":"C","html":"Object should be on the picture plane"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/83d31f31697bd30ee4fa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If the elevation of a point is 10 mm below reference line and plan is 5 mm above reference line, then the point will be in</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1<sup>st</sup> quadrant"},{"key":"B","html":"2<sup>nd</sup> quadrant"},{"key":"C","html":"3<sup>rd</sup> quadrant"},{"key":"D","html":"4<sup>st</sup> quadrant"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9c/9c563e63cb83b0262079.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8a/8a3301b7bf9a2ce1e0ba.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>“Vanishing point” is related to which type of projection</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Orthographic"},{"key":"B","html":"Oblique"},{"key":"C","html":"Isometric"},{"key":"D","html":"Perspective"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nProjections are converge at a vanishing point in perspective projection. This type of projection is<br />\nalso a pictorial view of object.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about sequential engineering?<br />\n1. Each stage of development process is carried out separately.<br />\n2. Next stage can’t start until the previous stage is finished.<br />\n3. Information flow is only in one direction.<br />\n4. Reduced product development cost.<br />\n5. This method is hardly used today.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3, 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 3, 5 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nSince, the information flow is only in one direction and it is not until the end of the chain that occurs changes and connections can be relayed to the start of the sequence, causing estimated cost to be under predicted.</p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In which of the following decision making model, probability of outcomes are unknown</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Decision under certainty"},{"key":"B","html":"Decision under uncertainty"},{"key":"C","html":"Decision under risk"},{"key":"D","html":"Decision under conflict"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n<strong>Decision under certainty:</strong> Each action results in known outcome with probability 1.<br />\n<strong>Decision under uncertainty:</strong> Each outcome has an assigned probability of occurrence.<br />\n<strong>Decision under risk:</strong> Each action results in multiple outcomes, with probability unknown.<br />\n<strong>Decision under conflict:</strong> Outcomes are replaced by course of action determined by opponent<br />\nwho is trying to maximize his/her objective function.</p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following may result in personal injury and property damage?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/56/569cc255b9c528633ee9.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 3, 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n<strong>Accident:</strong> Unplanned event resulting in injury and damage.<br />\n<strong>Incident:</strong> Unplanned event that may result is property damage but not involves personal injury.<br />\n<strong>Near miss:</strong> The accident, incident or event that did not happen.<br />\n<strong>Hazard:</strong> Any facility, location, equipment or action having potential of serious injury or death.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>What are the various techniques for liquid waste management?<br />\n1. Dewatering and sedimentation<br />\n2. Incineration<br />\n3. Root zone<br />\n4. Landfills<br />\n5. Composting</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3, 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3, 4, 5 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 3, 5 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5 only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n<strong>Dewatering/sedimentation:</strong> Extraction of water, so solid component left behind, which can be<br />\neasily removed.<br />\n<strong>Incineration:</strong> High temperature eliminated any solid component, leaving water free of<br />\nhazardous waste like scrap material, stag etc.<br />\n<strong>Root zone:</strong> Liquid waste coming from people (kitchen, bath water) water goes several steps<br />\nof processing like sedimentation tank, filter, polishing pond.<br />\n<strong>Landfills:</strong> Dumping of solid waste.<br />\n<strong>Composting:</strong> Transforming solid sediments into organic fertilizer.</p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Two different machine systems are shown with their reliability of equipment contain. Find which system is more safest and reliable.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/26cd558c7017a60c95c5.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only 1"},{"key":"B","html":"Only 2"},{"key":"C","html":"Both equally safe and reliable"},{"key":"D","html":"Can not be determined"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e7e456f486ff30dca580.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the types of quality is concerned with the requirement of specifications determined by the designer for the product?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Quality of performance"},{"key":"B","html":"Quality of conformance"},{"key":"C","html":"Quality of design"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nQuality of conformance means how the product or service meet the specifications determined by designer for a product.</p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A box container of 2000 bulb is subjected to acceptance sampling plan that examine 150 bulb part. Actual fraction defective is box = 0.03 and sampling plan has 0.53 probability of accepting a box of this quality. What is the average outgoing quality AOQ:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.0247"},{"key":"B","html":"0.0198"},{"key":"C","html":"0.0147"},{"key":"D","html":"0.147"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8a/8acabfc5e871c222af34.png\" /></div>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements<br />\n1. Pareto analysis uses Pareto’s law.<br />\n2. Pareto law is also known as 20-80 rule.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nPareto&rsquo;s law is also known as 80-20 law.</p>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following data: 12, 14, 10, 6, 8<br />\nWhat is the standard deviation for given numbers?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"40"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/47/47e3aff9004a84283cc7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"8"},{"key":"D","html":"10"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27f5b03b7c0e3160e831.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>With reference to Taguchi’s Noise factors which of the following statement are correct?<br />\n1. Design specifications come under Noise Factors.<br />\n2. Environmental specifications come under Noise Factors.<br />\n3. Quality specifications come under Noise Factors.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nDesign specification does not come under Noise factor.</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Six-sigma implies</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A statistical method"},{"key":"B","html":"A trouble-shooting method"},{"key":"C","html":"Teams are effective"},{"key":"D","html":"3.4 defects per million in outputs"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe defect level below which six-sigma operates is 3.4 defects per million opportunities.</p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The reliability block diagram of a system is shown in the following figure with the component reliability rated in each block. Find the system reliability.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/532042aae86435c32c86.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.670"},{"key":"B","html":"0.840"},{"key":"C","html":"0.753"},{"key":"D","html":"0.700"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>During radiography test, which region absorbs less radiation and transmits more.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Low and high density regions absorbs and transmit same amount of radiation."},{"key":"B","html":"High density region"},{"key":"C","html":"Low density region"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nLower density region of the component absorbs, less radiation and transmit more. Similarly, if higher density region of the component absorbs more radiation, it transmits less. Thus making the part of film less dark.<br />\nHence, on photographic plate, cracks appear as dark areas and inclusions appear as light region.</p>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A manager notes that his employee takes breaks every day. He takes at least eight minutes break each day and sometimes as long as an hour. Twenty minute breaks are more frequent than breaks of any other duration. If employee’s breaks were activity in PERT project, then while scheduling the project how much time would you allocate for the breaks?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"20 min"},{"key":"B","html":"29 min 20 sec"},{"key":"C","html":"60 min"},{"key":"D","html":"24 min 40 sec"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/67ad214aab135ee10f4c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/79ab790e488d83cfe6f3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Placing fake bids that benefits the seller of the item known as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Shell Bidding"},{"key":"B","html":"Fraud"},{"key":"C","html":"Auction sniping"},{"key":"D","html":"Shill Bidding"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Maturity value of annuity is given by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b0/b07bdb2778f5da3a4ecd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Where, <em>A</em> is annuity amount, <em>i</em> is interest rate and <em>n</em> is number of years.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In Earned Value Analysis (EVA), the difference between Earned Value (EV) and Actual Cast (AC) is called</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Cost variance"},{"key":"B","html":"Cost performance index"},{"key":"C","html":"Schedule variance"},{"key":"D","html":"Budget difference"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following depreciation methods results in higher depreciation in the initial years?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Straight line method"},{"key":"B","html":"Declining balance method"},{"key":"C","html":"Sinking fund method"},{"key":"D","html":"Sum-of-the-years-digits method"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>What are the diamond constrains of a project?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Time - Cost - Quality - Expectation"},{"key":"B","html":"Time - Cost - Scope - Quality"},{"key":"C","html":"Scope - Cost - Quality - Expectation"},{"key":"D","html":"Scope - Time - Quality - Expectation"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nTriple constrains = Time - Cost - Scope<br />\nDiamond constraints = Time - Cost - Scope - Quality<br />\nPMBok&rsquo;s constraints = Time - Cost - Scope - Quality - Risk - Resource</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following contracts is also called K-2 form of contract?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Lump-sum contract"},{"key":"B","html":"Cost plus fixed see contract"},{"key":"C","html":"Guaranteed maximum contract"},{"key":"D","html":"Unit price contract"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nUnit price contract : This is based on estimated quantities of the items involved in the work. The<br />\ncontractor is obligated to perform the quantities of the work actually required in the field at his<br />\nquoted prices, whether they are greater or less than the owner&rsquo;s estimates. This method is also<br />\ncalled K-2 form of contract.</p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. Free float is the difference between the total float and the head event slack.<br />\n2. Independent float is the difference between the total float and the tail event slack.<br />\n3. Slack is zero for critical activity.<br />\nWhich of the above statement(s) is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0cdf6541b7b962436f74.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding Tuckman’s model:<br />\n1. Team’s effectiveness initially decreases from forming to norming.<br />\n2. Team’s effectiveness is least in norming stage and highest in performing stage.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a0f8cb0dceb302e75309.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b2cff958341643b02b1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following crystallographic structure is most ductile?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Face centered cubic lattice (FCC)"},{"key":"B","html":"Body centered cubic lattice (BCC)"},{"key":"C","html":"Hexagonal close packed lattice (HCP)"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nFCC crystal structures exhibit more ductility (deform more readily under load before breaking)<br />\nthan materials with body-centered cubic (BCC) or hexagonal close-packed (HCP) crystal structures.</p>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following defect arises due to dislocation in material?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Volumetric defect"},{"key":"B","html":"Line defect"},{"key":"C","html":"Plane defect"},{"key":"D","html":"Point defect"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nDislocations are linear defects within a crystal&#39;s lattice structure where the regular, repeating<br />\narrangement of atoms is disrupted along a line. Thus, dislocations in materials refer to line<br />\ndefect.</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>How many kg of sulphur is required per 80 kg of final rubber product to completely cross-link butadiene (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>6</sub>) rubber?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"38.16 kg"},{"key":"B","html":"29.76 kg"},{"key":"C","html":"54.24 kg"},{"key":"D","html":"27.2 kg"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nAtomic weight of sulphur = 32<br />\nAtomic weight of C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>6</sub> = 12 &times; 4 + 6 &times; 1 = 54<br />\nFraction sulphur present in completely crosslinked product = 32&divide;(32 + 54) = 0.3721<br />\nThus, Amount of sulphur in 80 kg of final rubber = 0.3721 &times; 80</p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Perminvar alloy, having constant permeability is an alloy of</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Nickel, cobalt and iron"},{"key":"B","html":"Nickel, copper and zinc"},{"key":"C","html":"Copper, nickel and antimony"},{"key":"D","html":"Iron, zinc and bismuth"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nPerminvar alloy is an alloy of nickel (45%), cobalt (25%) and iron (30%). It is known for its<br />\nconstant magnetic permeability over a wide range of magnetic field strengths.</p>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consideration of creep is important in which of following applications?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Cycle chains"},{"key":"B","html":"Cast iron pipes"},{"key":"C","html":"Flywheel of steam engine"},{"key":"D","html":"Gas turbine blades"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe slow and continuous elongation of a material with time at constant stress and high temperature<br />\nbelow the elastic limit is called creep. Consideration of creep is crucial in Gas turbine blades<br />\nbecause they operate under continuous, high stress at very high temperatures.</p>"},{"n":66,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following elements impart hardness to steel?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54cb13394f03f2eb7284.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nChromium, Silicon and Nickel improve hardness of steel.</p>"},{"n":67,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which one of the following plots represents the B-H behaviour of a diamagnetic material?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/32/3295007537cdfe1bd340.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/121ffcc2e3ad37ffa162.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c6accea1748b98b1b5b1.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/591c81ab43b3c600fd90.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fc7bd091a135df79fc4b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":68,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If Hall voltage (<em>V<sub>H</sub></em>) of a metal and an n-type semiconductor sample are measured at room temperature where current <em>I<sub>x</sub></em>, applied magnetic field <em>B<sub>z</sub></em> and film dimensions are same for both samples, then which one of the following relations comparing hall voltage magnitude is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>V</em><sub><em>H</em>(metal)</sub> > <em>V</em><sub><em>H</em>(semiconductor)</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>V</em><sub><em>H</em>(metal)</sub> > <em>V</em><sub><em>H</em>(semiconductor)</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>V<sub>H</sub></em><sub>(metal)</sub> = <em>V</em><sub><em>H</em>(semiconductor)</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"No conclusion drawn"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33c4ca9e06f80bd6319c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":69,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The phenomenon that when a current is passed through the junction of two different metals, heat is absorbed or liberated depending upon the direction of current is known as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Seebeck effect"},{"key":"B","html":"Villari effect"},{"key":"C","html":"Peltier effect"},{"key":"D","html":"Kerr effect"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe Seebeck effect is the generation of a voltage across the two junctiont made of two dissimilar<br />\nconductors when the junctions are kept at a temperature difference, while the Peltier effect is the<br />\nabsorption or release of heat at the junction of two dissimilar conductors when an electric current<br />\n(I) is passed through them.</p>"},{"n":70,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A magnetic material has following characteristics:<br />\n1. It gets feebly magnetised in opposite direction of applied magnetic field.<br />\n2. Relative permeability, μ<em><sub>r</sub></em> is slightly less than 1.<br />\n3. In absence of external magnetic field, there are no permanent dipoles in the material.<br />\nThe nature of magnetic material is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Paramagnetic"},{"key":"B","html":"Diamagnetic"},{"key":"C","html":"Ferrimagnetic"},{"key":"D","html":"Ferromagnetic"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nDiamagnetic material properties are:<br />\n&bull; No permanent dipoles.<br />\n&bull; Gets feebly magnetized in opposite direction of applied magnetic field.<br />\n&bull; Relative permeability is slightly less than 1.<br />\nAlso, the susceptibility of paramagnetic material, ferromagnetic and ferrimagnetic materials is<br />\ngretaer than 1 i.e. they are magneitized in the direction of applied magnetic field.</p>"},{"n":71,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A coil is wound on a magnetic core and is excited from AC source initially. If the source voltage is tripled and frequency of supply from AC source is also tripled, the new value of eddy current loss in the core will become</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"three times the initial value."},{"key":"B","html":"nine times the initial value."},{"key":"C","html":"one third of the initial value."},{"key":"D","html":"same as the initial value."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d533ffa2fc3c7be33302.png\" /></div>"},{"n":72,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which one of the following materials is best suited as electrical insulating material?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Graphite"},{"key":"B","html":"Askarel"},{"key":"C","html":"Mercury"},{"key":"D","html":"Tin-antimony alloy"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Graphite is good electrical conductor.<br />\n&bull; Mercury is a goof conductor of electricity and used in thermometer manufacturing and other<br />\nchemical applications.<br />\n&bull; Tin-antimony alloy is used as soldering materials and is a good conductor.<br />\n&bull; Askerel has good electric insulation properties. It is generic term for a group of synthetic,<br />\nfire-resistant, chlorinated aromatic hydrocarbons used as electrical insulating liquids in<br />\ntransformers and capacitors, due to their excellent dielectric properties..</p>"},{"n":73,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the below given statements is not correct regarding soft ferromagnetic materials?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Soft ferromagnetic materials have low retentivity."},{"key":"B","html":"Soft ferromagnetic materials have high coercivity."},{"key":"C","html":"Soft ferromagnetic materials have narrow hysteresis loop."},{"key":"D","html":"Soft ferromagnetic materials are suitable for high frequency magnetic application."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nSoft ferromagnetic materials can be easily magnetized and demagnetized. Thus, they have small<br />\nvalue of coercivity i.e. they require a small magnetic field to demagnetize.</p>"},{"n":74,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The property among the given below, which changes in superconducting state of a material is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Crystal structure of material"},{"key":"B","html":"Specific heat of material"},{"key":"C","html":"Photo electric property"},{"key":"D","html":"Elastic property"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nSpecific heat suddenly increases and shows a discontinuous jump when a superconductor transit<br />\nfrom the normal state to the superconducting state. In the supeconducting state, the specific heat<br />\nis found to decrease with temperature exponentially. Other mentioned properties remain<br />\nunchanged in superconducting state of material.</p>"},{"n":75,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If |<em>A</em>|≠ 0 for the system <em>AX</em> = 0 ,then which of the following is false?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The system has unique solution"},{"key":"B","html":"The system has a zero solution"},{"key":"C","html":"The system has a trivial solution"},{"key":"D","html":"The system has a non - zero solution"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIn the homogeneous system of equations <em>AX</em> = 0, if |<em>A</em>| &ne; 0 then the system has a unique solution which is the trivial solution i.e. only zero solution.</p>"},{"n":76,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The system</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0cab483e202aae4616d3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>has</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"no solution"},{"key":"B","html":"unique solution"},{"key":"C","html":"infinitely many solutions"},{"key":"D","html":"two linearly independent solutions"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/201f437138f5310c5f44.png\" /></div>\n</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/998cd53c99fcbaa28d13.png\" /></div>"},{"n":77,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em> = <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/605feeda009d0379302f.png\" /> Then the number of linearly independent Eigen vectors of <em>A</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c1c6186ebfb2b3462f05.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/dd7cea53c54f8ee467bf.png\" /></div>\n</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":78,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Rolle’s theorem cannot be applied for the function <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) = |<em>x</em>| in [–2, 2] because</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) is not continuous in [–2, 2]"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) is not continuous in (–2, 2)"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em> (–2) ≠ <em>f</em> (2)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f7a9b708720d9ead1bda.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":79,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following improper integrals is divergent?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fc9be3cb3f371461428.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a0393a99bb84bdad0d78.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7a84caf5465bf451de32.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/1609f5972d660d5f0a3e.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03f759bb3bd766e0be2f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cf/cf4d89ed525554fc0043.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":80,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The area bounded by the parabola y<sup>2</sup> = 4<em>ax</em> and its latus rectum is____</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e95f13db25673bf435c7.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f568c419beee48aad3d6.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/7186454a9c869566d9cf.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d1e8309bdc630f4592c.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2e40c616b8e47c7d64c2.png\" /></div>\n</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":81,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The directional derivative of x<sup>2</sup>y<sup>2</sup>z<sup>2</sup> at the point (1, 1, –1) in the direction of the tangent to the curve <em>x</em> = <em>e<sup>t</sup></em>, y = sin2<em>t</em> + 1, z = 1 – cost at <em>t</em> = 0 is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/01e346925df4c6458605.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/56/567d4b1a5dd3b5675956.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f058d4973f8dc11dfa1d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3f40f61adfcef4b3bd28.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a70c3e0a4e2f25c04bf1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f80b1e1841bd3b07cfe8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":82,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>BLOCKED : YOLXPVW : : ? : OZFMXS</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"LAUNCH"},{"key":"B","html":"DEBATE"},{"key":"C","html":"LABOUR"},{"key":"D","html":"RESULT"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5a4c23cdd302832af8d9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":83,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Find out the wrong term in the following series:<br />\n2, 3, 4, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 16</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3"},{"key":"B","html":"6"},{"key":"C","html":"9"},{"key":"D","html":"12"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe given sequence is a combination of three series:<br />\n2, 4, 9, ..................<br />\n3, 6, 12, ..................<br />\n4, 8, 16, ..................<br />\nEach term is twice the preceding term, so 9 is wrong and must be replaced by 8.</p>"},{"n":84,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Study the letter-series given below and answer the below question:<br />\nH D Y S M W N B Q P O C R T B L Z V E G U F<br />\nWhich two neighbours in the given arrangement are farthest in the alphabetical order?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"B and Q"},{"key":"B","html":"D and Y"},{"key":"C","html":"U and F"},{"key":"D","html":"V and E"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nClearly, D and Y are neighbours in the given series and are separated by the maximum number of<br />\nletters, i.e., 20 in the english alphabet.</p>"},{"n":85,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The number of zeros in the below product is<br />\n1<sup>1</sup> × 2<sup>2</sup> × 3<sup>3</sup> × 4<sup>4</sup> × _ _ _ _ _ 98<sup>98</sup> × 99<sup>99</sup> × 100<sup>100</sup></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1200"},{"key":"B","html":"1300"},{"key":"C","html":"1050"},{"key":"D","html":"1225"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/7263e143c4f47d2f9113.png\" /></div>"},{"n":86,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>(<em>x</em><sup>29</sup> – <em>x</em><sup>25</sup> + <em>x</em><sup>13</sup> – 1) is divisible by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(<em>x</em> + 1) but not by (<em>x</em> – 1)"},{"key":"B","html":"(<em>x</em> – 1) but not by (<em>x</em> + 1)"},{"key":"C","html":"Both (<em>x</em> + 1) and (<em>x</em> – 1)"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither (<em>x</em> – 1) not (<em>x</em> + 1)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e412a632c35a6289c54.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/273656f80c322d88c1e9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":87,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77858cc0b62804b49b60.png\" /> then</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9feb4f7cc1d1fa0ede5d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02378ccdcce501882c15.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b1f708234fe16c94861.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b898a4940bb004e90c1c.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb8f3f890a442f7e6c4c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":88,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A trader mixes some freely available drinking water with some quantity of pure milk that is procured from a reliable dairy and he sells out the mixture at 20% discount. If the quantity of water is 20% to that of pure milk, what is his overall profit/loss (%)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4%, loss"},{"key":"B","html":"4%, Profit"},{"key":"C","html":"No profit No loss"},{"key":"D","html":"4.4%, profit"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c75f0535ed4e8fe256a4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/6471987f0c31f425290e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":89,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A dishonest dealer purchases goods at 20% discount of the cost price of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5dc67948148bf2f73a36.png\" /> and also cheats his wholesaler by getting 20% extra through false weighing, per kg. Then, he marks up his goods by 80% of x, but he gives a discount of 25% besides he cheats his customer by weighing 10% less than the required. What is his overall profit percentage?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"125%"},{"key":"B","html":"98.66%"},{"key":"C","html":"100%"},{"key":"D","html":"120%"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/556edd01870de1fa83a0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":90,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The ratio of time taken to run a certain distance by Sourabh and Pawan is 4 : 3 and thus Pawan wins the race by 360 m. What is the distance of race course?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"360 m"},{"key":"B","html":"720 m"},{"key":"C","html":"1440 m"},{"key":"D","html":"2880 m"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1b/1bc9f9885d4959b028ac.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d411c2edf021a10cac10.png\" /></div>"},{"n":91,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Three cubes each of edge 3 cm long are placed together as shown in the adjoining figure. The surface area of the cuboid so formed is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/32/326ef216306f282eb11b.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"182 cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"162 cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"126 cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/12a1458567cf9773bbb0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":92,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> In a non-turnkey contracts the work of fixing reinforcement of steel bars can be taken as a work package and can be assigned to one contractor while laying of cement concrete can be assigned to some other contractor as a separate work package.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> In non-turnkey contracts, the total project is divided into suitable work packages such that responsibility for poor performance, if any, can be easily attributed to a particular contractor.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIf the work is assigned as given in statement I, it will become difficult to identify the contractor who has not carried out the work as per specifications, if it is found at a later stage that the RCC construction has defects.<br />\nThus, laying of cement concrete and fixing of reinforcement in the form of steel bars of the given<br />\ndesign and specifications are to be treated as one work package.</p>"},{"n":93,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> A blockchain is an anonymous online ledger that uses data structure to simplify the way we transact.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The block time is the average time it takes for the network to generate one extra block in the blockchain.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":94,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The Laser printers beams the print onto a metal drum.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Laser printers work by using a heated wire to positively charge a drum, which is then passed over by a laser that reverses the charge in the areas that it hits.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nLaser printer works by charging a drum with a heated wire and then using a laser to selectively<br />\ndischarge areas that represent the image. The toner sticks to discharged areas and is transferred<br />\nto paper. Thus, Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true and Statement (II) is the correct<br />\nexplanation of Statement (I).</p>"},{"n":95,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> An ethical dilemma occurs when a decision is required but different values seem to be in conflict.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> In ethical dilemma, the required course of action is clear.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nAn ethical dilemma is a type of ethical issue that arises when the available choices and obligations in a specific situation do not allow for an ethical outcome.</p>"},{"n":96,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The rules of ethics are also called moral law.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Laws are always enforced by the government under the threat of punishment.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nLaws are not always necessarily enforced by the government under the threat of punishment.</p>"},{"n":97,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Inconel contains 80% nickel, 14% chromium and 6% iron<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Inconel is a family of austenitic nickel-chromium-based superalloys.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nInconel consists of 80% Nickel, 14% chromium and 6% iron. It is a family of austenitic nickelchromium-<br />\nbased superalloys. Inconel alloys are oxidation-corrosion resistant materials well suited<br />\nfor service in extreme environments subjected to pressure and heat.</p>"},{"n":98,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Algae, fungi and simple plants are the first organisms to colonize an area in secondary succession.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Secondary succession occurs when an area that has previously had an ecological community is so disturbed or changed that the original community was destroyed and a new community moves in.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAlgae, fungi and simple plants are the first organisms to colonize an area in primary succession.</p>"},{"n":99,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> The addition of Carbonic acid in the ocean is known as acidification of ocean.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> When atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> is added in the sea water, chemical reaction occurs that reduces pH value of ocean water by the formation of Carbonic acid.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":100,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • CIVIL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Each of the next items consists of two statements, one labelled as the ‘Statement (I)’ and the other as ‘Statement (II)’. You are to examine these two statements carefully and select the answers to these items using the codes given below:<br />\n<strong>Codes:</strong><br />\n(a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is NOT the correct explanation of Statement (I).<br />\n(c) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.<br />\n(d) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Producer’s and consumer’s risk can be defined on the basis of Type-I errors and Type-II errors.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Type-I error is to accept the lot when the true value of the quality characteristic is RQL and Type-II error is associated with the producer’s point to reject when the true value of the quality characteristic is AQL.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nType-II error is to accept the lot when the true value of the quality characteristic is AQL and<br />\nType-I error is associated with the producer&rsquo;s point to reject when the true value of the quality<br />\ncharacteristic is RQL.</p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":100}],"stats":{"questions":100,"maxMarks":200,"types":{"MCQ":100},"withoutKey":0}};
